<p><strong>By: Christine Rauer; Date: 12 November 2026.</strong></p>

<p><em>In the autumn of 2026, two members of our <a
href="https://emergence.leiden.edu/get-involved#advisory-board">international
advisory board</a>, Christine Rauer and Kees Dekker, journeyed to Water
Stratford, Buckinghamshire. Here, they uncovered (quite literally) the
grave of one of the most important Old English philologists of the long
nineteenth century: rev. Joseph Bosworth (1788–1876). In this short blog
post, Christine Rauer reports their experiences, along with a number of
photographs.</em></p>
<figure>
<img src="assets/images/blog/BosworthGrave1.jpg">
</figure>
<p>Bosworth was appointed Rector of St Giles in Water Stratford in 1858,
when he was 70 years old, and in the same year as being appointed
Rawlinsonian Professor of Anglo-Saxon at Oxford. Water Stratford is a
small village some 20 miles north of Oxford; the post came with a
Rectory for him to live in, but Bosworth also kept a house and and
office in Oxford. He is buried in the churchyard of St Giles, together
with his second wife, Anne. Having read about Water Stratford in Dabney
Bankert's biography, <em>Philology in Turbulent Times: Joseph Bosworth,
His Dictionary and the Recovery of Old English</em> (2023), Kees and I
wanted to visit his grave. The two photos above show the interior of the
small church St Giles when we got there, with the pulpit from which
Bosworth must have preached.</p>
<figure>
<img src="assets/images/blog/BosworthGrave2.jpg">
</figure>
<p>The church is an old one, with a Norman doorway, but was restructured
in the nineteenth century. The churchyard is very badly overgrown, and
we had trouble finding the grave.</p>
<figure>
<img src="assets/images/blog/BosworthGrave3.jpg">
</figure>
<p>We had read that the grave was on the northern part of the
churchyard, and in the end we thought that it might be the overgrown one
with iron railings around it, in the background of the photo. Apparently
Bosworth had the grave (initially of his wife) positioned in such a way
that it could be seen from the Rectory. He was then later, in June 1876,
buried there himself.</p>
<figure>
<img src="assets/images/blog/BosworthGrave4.jpg">
</figure>
<p>It took us some time gently to remove the thick layer of ivy and soil
that was on the tombstone. The inscription is beautifully made and was
still very legible underneath it. It was sad to see the entire
churchyard so neglected and overgrown.</p>
<figure>
<img src="assets/images/blog/BosworthGrave5.jpg">
</figure>
<p>The visit made a great impression on us. We had got to see more of
the life of one of the great nineteenth-century Old English scholars,
and learned more about the world he lived and worked in. But it also
became clear how vulnerable the material remains of that world really
are, and how ephemeral the things turned out to be that must have meant
something to Joseph Bosworth. It makes his scholarly work even more
important, as that survives well taken care of, in modern libraries and
online.</p>
<p><em>In May 2026, the EMERGENCE project co-organized a memorial event
on the occasion of the 150th anniversary of Bosworth’s death (see <a
href="https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/events/2026/05/bosworth-memorial">here</a>).
During the event, attended by 100+ scholars online, a variety of
speakers (Christine and Kees included) reflected on the impact of
Bosworth and his</em> A Dictionary of the Anglo-Saxon Language<em>,
which today forms the basis for the most widely used <a
href="https://bosworthtoller.com/">online dictionary of Old
English</a>.</em></p>

